 "youtube": {
  "title": "Battle of Stamford Bridge: Why Vikings Fought Without Armour",
  "description": "The Battle of Stamford Bridge in 1066 was lost before it began: the Norwegian army had left its mail armour at the ships. So how did one hot morning decide who ruled England?\n\nIn September 1066, Harald Hardrada of Norway took York. The sagas say the day was so hot that his men left their mail shirts with the fleet at Riccall. Then King Harold Godwinson arrived after a forced march of some 185 miles, and Hardrada was killed. Three days later, William of Normandy landed at Pevensey, and on 14 October Harold's tired army lost England at the Battle of Hastings.\n\nSubscribe to Stickstory for a true history story every day. Would you have fought without armour on a hot day? Tell me in the comments!\n\n#shorts #stamfordbridge #1066 #stickstory",
